{
  format: 1
  name: surface-view-parameter-without-anchor-invalid
  suite: static
  spec: ["#interfaces", "§10.1", "#mutations", "§8.3"]
  tags: ["typing"]
  note: '''
    The fail-to-explicit half of §10.1's inferred view parameter. Inference is
    "exactly as a mutation parameter is (§8.3)": a use anchors a type only where
    the position supplies one — a comparison against a typed field, a
    key-selector position, a typed function argument. Neither `@tag` nor
    `@other` occupies such a position here: `tag: @tag` is a PROJECTION member,
    which names an output and copies the parameter's value without constraining
    it, and `@other == @tag` relates two parameters to each other and to no typed
    value, so the pair is satisfied by every type.

    §10.1 pins that as a static load error, and pins what the diagnostic must
    say: it "requests an explicit declaration: declare the parameter in `$params`
    with its type." A silently `json`-widened parameter would put an untyped
    value in the external wire contract, which is exactly what the rule forbids.
  '''

  package: {
    $liasse: 1
    $app: "t.viewanchor@1.0.0"
    $model: {
      tasks: {
        $key: "id"
        id: "text"
        done: "bool"
      }
      $public: {
        tagged: {
          // No $params, and no typed position to infer from: `tag: @tag` is a
          // projection member and `@other == @tag` compares two parameters.
          $view: ".tasks[:t | @other == @tag] { id, tag: @tag }"
        }
      }
    }
  }

  expect: {
    outcome: invalid
    violates: ["#interfaces", "§10.1"]
    detail: "the surface `$view` does not constrain `@tag`/`@other` to a single type; the diagnostic must request an explicit declaration — declare the parameter in `$params` with its type (§10.1)"
  }
}
